  "problem_statement": "# Missing `tick` and `move_to` methods in `TickingDateTimeFactory` class\n\nWhen using freezegun with `tick=True`, the `tick()` and `move_to()` methods are not available, causing AttributeError exceptions when trying to use them.\n\n## Description\n\nI'm trying to use the ticking functionality of freezegun to manipulate time in my tests. When I create a frozen time with `tick=True` and try to use the `tick()` or `move_to()` methods, I get an AttributeError.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nimport time\nfrom freezegun import freeze_time\n\n# Create a frozen time with tick=True\nwith freeze_time('Jan 14th, 2012, 23:59:59', tick=True) as ft:\n    # Try to use the tick method\n    ft.tick(61)  # This raises AttributeError\n    \n    # Or try to use the move_to method\n    ft.move_to('Jan 15th, 2012, 00:59:59')  # This also raises AttributeError\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe `tick()` and `move_to()` methods should be available when using freezegun with `tick=True`, allowing me to manually adjust the frozen time while still having it tick forward automatically.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\n```\nAttributeError: 'TickingDateTimeFactory' object has no attribute 'tick'\n```\n\nor \n\n```\nAttributeError: 'TickingDateTimeFactory' object has no attribute 'move_to'\n```\n\nIt seems like these methods are defined for other factory classes but not for the `TickingDateTimeFactory` class."
}